            "public_requirement": "Two transistor-level input TIA branches with DC-overload cancellation must accept inp and inn and preserve the specified input bias and overload behavior.",
            "electrical_reason": "The two common-base/cascode TIA paths are physically present: inp/inn feed the emitters of XQcbp/XQcbn, their collectors feed XQcsp/XQcsn, and Rcp/Rcn provide collector loads at tiap/tian. However, the asserted DC cancellation sinks XMtp/XMtn have drain terminals at inp/inn and gates gtp/gtn derived resistively from tiap/tian. An increased positive input current increases common-base collector current and lowers tiap/tian through Rcp/Rcn; that lowers gtp/gtn and reduces the respective NMOS input sink current. Thus the supposed cancellation path responds in the same direction as the overload error rather than increasing shunt cancellation current. The favorable overload input-voltage result can arise from the intrinsic low common-base emitter impedance, but does not establish a coherent DC-overload-cancellation loop."
